  "why": "The statement asks for a forbidden-subgraph characterisation of all word-representable graphs; it names no subclass, so every forbidden-subgraph characterisation of a restricted class (split graphs with independent set size 4, co-bipartite with a clique of size at most 4, all split graphs, all co-bipartite graphs, graphs with an all-adjacent vertex) is a case. Co-bipartite results stated as orientation or vertex-ordering conditions rather than forbidden subgraphs answer a changed question and are variant, as are the neighbouring results on word lengths, square-freeness, circulants, line graphs, melon graphs, representation numbers and generalised representability. Censuses and individual certificates are instances. Roy-Hariharasubramanian and Chen-Hameed-Kitaev are accepted by journals of record, so digested; the Pith AI-referee objections are automated and neither digest nor knock down (F8). The December 2025 Srinivasan preprints are unreviewed and doubtful because the later journal paper from an overlapping team still treats the split list as open. Hefty et al. has only a 2024 year date, so after_snapshot is null. Pith reviews are the only AI involvement, and they criticise, so ai_role is none throughout.",
